        {
          "id": "spf:sprint-goals",
          "name": "Sprint Goals"
        }
      ],
      "summaryOfTheGaps": "The good news is you are not starting from scratch. Strengths like Backlog Management, Prioritization, and Process Management carry straight over and give you a real head start. What is newer is the craft at the center of it, things like Problem Statements, Vision Formation, and Kanban Board Management. You will also grow into the everyday work, like Feature Vision Definition and Feature Scope Definition. Just as important is how the work happens on a team. Practices like Shared Ownership, Empowerment, and Shared Leadership are less about tools and more about how you show up with the people around you. You will not learn all of this up front. Like the work itself, you grow into it a piece at a time, taking on a little more as you go.",
      "trainingRecommendations": "There is no single right path, but here is one sensible way to grow into it:\n1. Start with Vision Formation, Backlog Management, and Kanban Board Management. These are the first skills to build, and getting them solid makes everything after it easier.\n2. Get a feel for how teams actually run the work by learning a method like Google Design Sprint Method and Scrum Method.\n3. Practice the real tasks on something small, like Feature Vision Definition and Feature Scope Definition, rather than only reading about them.\n4. Pick up the common tools as you need them, like Notion, Figjam, and Zoom, learning each one on a real task.\n5. Build a couple of real examples you can show, like Acceptance Criteria and Competitive Analysis, to prove the skills to yourself and others.\n6. Grow the team habits this work runs on, like Shared Ownership and Empowerment, by using them with an actual team.\n\nThrough all of it, lean on what you already do well, like Backlog Management and Prioritization. Those strengths give you something to stand on while the newer skills catch up.",
